    "id": "1-introduction-the-map-vs-territory-problem-in-somatic-science"
  }, "1. Introduction: The Map vs. Territory Problem in Somatic Science"), mdx("p", null, "Over the past two decades, Polyvagal Theory, formulated by Dr. Stephen Porges, has become the dominant scientific framework in somatic psychology, trauma therapy, and body-based healing. The theory's division of the autonomic nervous system into three evolutionary stages\u2014the ancient unmyelinated dorsal vagus (immobilization/freeze), the sympathetic system (mobilization/fight-flight), and the uniquely mammalian myelinated ventral vagus (social engagement/safety)\u2014provides a compelling, easy-to-understand model for clinical practitioners."), mdx("p", null, "However, within mainstream evolutionary biology, comparative neuroanatomy, and physiology, Polyvagal Theory faces significant scientific critique:"), mdx("ul", null, mdx("li", {
    parentName: "ul"
  }, mdx("strong", {
    parentName: "li"
  }, "Phylogenetic Inaccuracies:"), " Evolutionary biologists contest Porges' assertion that the myelinated vagal system is a late, uniquely mammalian adaptation. Reptiles and amphibians possess myelinated vagal fibers, and both mammalian branches of the vagus are present in primitive vertebrates."), mdx("li", {
    parentName: "ul"
